# tests/tcyr/jpeg_noninterleaved.tcyr — T.81 § A.2 non-interleaved JPEG scans
# (0.6.0). Standalone CPU suite (own main + assert_summary), like the other
# tests/tcyr/*.tcyr; fixtures are embedded byte arrays, no file I/O.
#
# WHAT THIS SUITE IS FOR. Before 0.6.0 chitra decoded only the INTERLEAVED
# layout: a one-component frame whose SOF0 declared H > 1 or V > 1 was rejected
# with CHITRA_ERR_UNSUPPORTED rather than mis-rendered, and one whose H·V
# exceeded 10 was rejected with CHITRA_ERR_JPEG_SOF by a cap T.81 § B.2.3
# conditions on Ns > 1. Both classes are spec-legal baseline JPEG that
# libjpeg writes on request. 0.6.0 decodes them.
#
# THE ORACLE, and why it is unusually strong here. All four grayscale fixtures
# below were produced by libjpeg-turbo 3.2.0 from ONE source image and differ
# from each other in EXACTLY ONE BYTE — the SOF0 sampling nibble at offset 100
# (0x11 / 0x21 / 0x22 / 0x44) — and `djpeg` decodes all four to the identical
# PGM. So the reference-correct decode of an H>1 grayscale JPEG is provably its
# H=1 twin's decode, established by an external encoder AND an external decoder
# rather than by chitra agreeing with itself. Every expected value below was
# read out of `djpeg -nosmooth`; chitra matches it over all 1536 bytes, and for
# the grayscale cells ImageMagick agrees byte-for-byte as an independent second
# oracle.
#
# NOTE on ImageMagick and SUBSAMPLED JPEG: it is NOT a valid second oracle for
# the interleaved 4:2:0 fixture. ImageMagick upsamples chroma with a fancy
# (triangle) filter where chitra and `djpeg -nosmooth` use box, so it differs
# in 937 of 1536 bytes at up to 40/byte on that file — a filter difference, not
# a decode bug. Use `djpeg -nosmooth` for any subsampled JPEG cell.
#
# REGENERATION. The source images are generated (not downloaded) so the corpus
# is reproducible; the pattern varies in BOTH axes and gives each 8x8 block a
# distinct DC level, so a block placed in the wrong grid cell changes the
# asserted pixels rather than hiding in an equal sum.
#
#   python3 - <<'EOF'
#   w, h = 24, 16
#   g = bytearray(); r = bytearray()
#   for y in range(h):
#       for x in range(w):
#           bx, by = x // 8, y // 8
#           g.append(min(255, 30 + 70*bx + 45*by + (x%8)*7 + (y%8)*3))
#           r.append(min(255, 20 + 80*bx + (x%8)*6))
#           r.append(min(255, 20 + 90*by + (y%8)*5))
#           r.append(min(255, 200 - 60*bx - 30*by))
#   open('g2.pgm','wb').write(b'P5\n24 16\n255\n' + bytes(g))
#   open('r2.ppm','wb').write(b'P6\n24 16\n255\n' + bytes(r))
#   EOF
#   cjpeg -quality 80 -grayscale -sample 1x1 -outfile G_1x1.jpg g2.pgm   # 372 B
#   cjpeg -quality 80 -grayscale -sample 2x1 -restart 1B -outfile G_rst.jpg g2.pgm
#   printf '0: 0 63 0 0;\n1: 0 63 0 0;\n2: 0 63 0 0;\n' > seq.scan
#   printf '0: 0 63 0 0;\n1,2: 0 63 0 0;\n'             > part.scan
#   cjpeg -quality 80 -sample 2x2,1x1,1x1                  -outfile IL3.jpg  r2.ppm
#   cjpeg -quality 80 -sample 2x2,1x1,1x1 -scans seq.scan  -outfile MS.jpg   r2.ppm
#   cjpeg -quality 80 -sample 2x2,1x1,1x1 -scans part.scan -outfile PART.jpg r2.ppm
#
# The H>1 grayscale fixtures are NOT stored separately: fx_jpeg_ni_gray_hv
# patches the one differing byte, which is what makes the single-byte-difference
# property a checked invariant of this suite rather than a claim in a comment.
include "src/lib.cyr"
include "lib/assert.cyr"

# ================================================================
# Fixtures
# ================================================================

# The control: 24x16 grayscale, SOF0 sampling 1x1, one interleaved-by-degeneracy
# scan. Decodes on every chitra since 0.3.0, and its decode is the reference
# answer for every H/V variant below.
